            <ArticleTitle>Giant Right Intrathoracic Myxoid Fusocellular Lipoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>302189</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2015/302189</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intrathoracic lipomas are rare benign tumors; their behavior is not completely clear and their surgical removal may be challenging. We report a case of a giant right intrathoracic myxoid fusocellular lipoma compressing the lung, tracheobronchial tree, and esophagus which was removed through a posterolateral thoracotomy. Complete removal resulted in resolution of the chest pain and improvement of the dyspnea, with no recurrence at 4-year follow-up.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
